  "In the context of scale development, what is the significance of establishing convergent and discriminant validity, and how are these evaluated using Confirmatory Factor Analysis (CFA)?\nA. Convergent validity ensures all items measure the same construct, evaluated by the average variance extracted (AVE); discriminant validity ensures constructs are distinct, evaluated by comparing AVE with the squared correlations between constructs.\nB. Convergent validity ensures items measure different constructs, evaluated by Cronbach's alpha; discriminant validity ensures all items measure the same construct, evaluated by factor loadings.\nC. Convergent validity ensures items are reliable, evaluated by test-retest method; discriminant validity ensures the scale is stable over time, evaluated by comparing mean scores.\nD. Convergent validity ensures the scale measures what it intends to measure, evaluated by face validity; discriminant validity ensures the scale does not measure what it should not measure, evaluated by expert judgment.\nA",
